mzLibR licensing
================

mzLibR itself is licensed **LGPL-3.0-or-later**, as declared in DESCRIPTION
(`License: LGPL (>= 3)`). The full texts are installed with the package in
`inst/licenses/`: LGPL-3.0.txt, and GPL-3.0.txt, which the LGPL extends.

Why LGPL and not something more permissive
------------------------------------------

mzLibR is a binding to mzLib, which is LGPL-3.0-or-later, and the bridge
executable this package runs contains mzLib in compiled form. Distributing that
binary — whether bundled, cached by `mzlibr_install_bridge()`, or built from
pyMzLib — is distributing mzLib, so mzLibR is licensed to match.

This is not a hypothetical. The sibling Rust binding initially declared MIT,
which would have been a real violation, and it was corrected before release. A
binding cannot be more permissive than the library it binds.

The bridge override is a licence affordance, not just a convenience
-------------------------------------------------------------------

`options(mzlibr.bridge=)` and the `MZLIB_BRIDGE` environment variable let a user
point mzLibR at a bridge executable they built themselves. LGPL-3.0 section 4
requires that a work using the library be distributed in a form that permits the
user to relink it against a modified version of that library. For a package that
invokes the library as a subprocess, replacing the executable *is* relinking, so
these two overrides are how mzLibR satisfies that clause. They are documented in
`?mzlibr_bridge_path` and named in the error raised when no bridge is found, and
they must not be removed.

Third-party components carried by the bridge executable
-------------------------------------------------------

The bridge is not distributed inside this source package — it is staged,
downloaded, or pointed at. When present it is a self-contained .NET binary that
carries the following, each under its own licence:

  mzLib                     LGPL-3.0-or-later   https://github.com/smith-chem-wisc/mzLib
  .NET runtime & libraries  MIT                 https://github.com/dotnet/runtime
  Newtonsoft.Json           MIT                 https://github.com/JamesNK/Newtonsoft.Json
  Intel libmmd              Intel Simplified Software License (redistributable)

Reference data compiled into or read by mzLib:

  Unimod                    Unimod terms of use
  PSI-MOD                   CC BY 4.0
  UniProt                   CC BY 4.0

mzLibR contains none of these in its own sources. It contains R code only, and
the licence texts above.
